   10. Permitted Personal, Internal and Creative Use.

      Subject to this complete License, You may obtain, inspect, run,
      reproduce and modify the Work, and self-host it for personal use,
      private noncommercial group use, or use within Your organization,
      including use in Your business. No software license fee is payable
      for those uses. The number of employees, internal users, Studios,
      Projects or Spaces alone does not require additional authorization.
      Remote access over the Internet does not by itself make an internal
      deployment an external platform service.

      Unless separately authorized by the Producer in writing under
      Section 11, an instance You operate must use Self-Deployment Mode.
      "Self-Deployment Mode" means an operating configuration in which
      in-application credit purchases or top-ups, paid memberships,
      subscriptions and other features for charging users for access to
      the Work or its platform functionality are disabled. You must not
      activate, restore, recreate or expose those paid-operation features,
      including by modifying code or bypassing the mode restrictions.
      This condition applies even if an enabled payment feature has not
      yet collected money. An authorized commercial deployment may enable
      such features only within its separate written authorization.

      Self-Deployment Mode does not require payment to unlock internal
      user, Studio, Project or Space entitlements. Roles, permissions and
      technical capacity may still be configured for operational needs;
      their existence is not a requirement to buy a hosted membership.
      Paying a hosting or model provider for resources, or charging for
      creative works or genuine services permitted by this section, does
      not by itself enable or authorize the paid-operation features above.

      Internal use includes access by Your employees and contractors
      genuinely performing work for Your organization. A contractor may
      install, maintain or operate a customer's dedicated instance on
      that customer's behalf, including for a fee, provided access is
      confined to the customer's otherwise permitted use. Merely calling
      customers employees, members, invitees or contractors does not
      convert external platform operation into internal use.

      You may create, commission, edit, distribute and sell creative
      works, including films, advertisements, images, audio, 3D assets
      and other deliverables, and charge for the creative work or Your
      creative services. You may invite a client to review, comment on
      or contribute to a specific creative project You are producing
      for that client, provided You are not selling independent platform
      access or platform-use entitlements to that client.

      A fee for creative output or for genuine installation, maintenance,
      training or support does not by itself constitute a platform access
      fee. The substance of what is provided, not its billing label or
      whether the installation is single-tenant, determines whether
      Section 11 applies. Redistribution remains subject to Section 4.

   11. Separate Authorization for External Commercial Platform Services.

      Without a separate written authorization from the Producer, You
      may not use the Work or a Derivative Work to provide an External
      Commercial Platform Service.

      "External Commercial Platform Service" means making the Work, or
      a substantial set of its platform features, available to persons
      outside the permitted personal, private-group or internal uses
      in Section 10 for their own use, where that access is offered for
      a commercial purpose or in return for direct or indirect economic
      consideration. This includes browser access, API access and access
      through another application or an agent when the substance of the
      offering is access to those platform features. Merely producing
      and delivering creative works or operating the Work internally
      does not meet this definition.

      Examples of consideration include subscriptions, access or seat
      fees, prepaid credits or usage charges, required purchases or paid
      memberships that unlock access, bundled or offline payments, and
      advertising or sponsorship revenue tied to providing the platform.
      A free tier or trial forming part of such an offering is included.
      A genuinely noncommercial public deployment is not prohibited by
      this section merely because it is publicly accessible or displays
      the attribution required by Section 12. The other applicable
      conditions of this License, including Section 12, still apply.

      This condition applies whether the service uses one workspace or
      many, is publicly advertised or invitation-only, uses the Producer's
      model services or another provider, or is supplied through a proxy,
      reseller or other intermediary. Disabling payment features,
      collecting payment elsewhere, or relabeling paid service credits
      as gifts does not authorize the underlying restricted activity.

      Operating an authorized service under the Breatic brand requires
      the scope of authorization and any co-branding to be confirmed in
      writing. Retaining the Breatic marks alone is not authorization.
      Running a service on Your own servers does not by itself authorize
      a paid-operation mode. Fees and other commercial terms are agreed
      separately; this License neither sets a revenue-share rate nor
      creates an automatic right
      to operate by paying a particular amount.

   12. Preservation of Breatic Identity and Notices.

      Preserve existing copyright, license and attribution notices in the
      Work and its documentation. When You run or redistribute an interface
      supplied with or derived from the Work, or provide an interface under
      Your control through which users access the Work or a substantial
      set of its platform features, keep the Breatic name and supplied logo
      visible and reasonably prominent in the application identity area
      and retain access to the applicable license and attribution notices.
      This applies to an independently developed interface connected by
      API or another protocol, whether or not that interface is itself a
      Derivative Work. Do not remove, obscure, replace or alter the marks
      or notices to present the Work as an unbranded or independently
      originated platform.

      You may translate interface text and documentation and adapt layout
      for language, screen size or accessibility, while keeping the Latin
      name "Breatic", the logo's identity and the substance of the notices.
      Notices may be relocated only to an equivalently prominent and
      accessible location. A genuinely machine-to-machine use with no
      user-facing interface need not add a graphical interface, but must
      preserve notices in distributed software and documentation and must
      not falsely claim independent origin. An interface You provide to
      users is not exempt merely because the connected backend is headless.
      An interface that only presents creative outputs, without providing
      access to the Work's platform features, does not require Breatic
      branding solely because those outputs were created with the Work.

      To the extent the Producer may grant such rights, You receive a
      limited, non-exclusive permission to display the Breatic name and
      supplied logo as necessary to comply with this License, including
      for permitted modified deployments. Identify material modifications
      and the actual operator without implying that an
      unapproved deployment is an official or authorized partner service.

      No other trademark permission is granted. Using the Breatic marks
      as Your own service's trade identity, rather than identifying the
      software as required above, requires separate written permission.
      Co-branding, white labeling, and registration of domains, accounts
      or names containing Breatic or confusingly similar marks likewise
      require separate written permission, except for lawful descriptive
      reference. Required software identification does not by itself
      constitute such a service name or claim of partnership.
      The Producer may expressly approve a different branding arrangement
      in writing. Removing the marks does not remove Section 11.

      This section does not require Breatic branding or watermarks on
      original creative works made using the software. Software or
      documentation actually copied into an output retains its applicable
      notices and license conditions. Third-party attribution duties
      are not waived.

